PUBLIC RESPONSE: {E} 

The strongest case against E qualifying rests on the 50/50 ownership structure and the look-through requirement in the corporate definition. Under NI 45-106, when a corporation does not itself clear the $5 M net-asset threshold on a stand-alone basis after netting the $300 k investment-property liabilities against the $5.5 M GIC, the analysis shifts to whether every beneficial owner meets an individual AI test. Alex’s $900 k in net financial assets falls short of the $1 M financial-asset test, and Bob’s income and negative net-worth position are well below any income or asset threshold. Because neither owner satisfies an individual test, the entity cannot rely on the “all owners are AIs” route either.  

By contrast, the facts in A can still be read to satisfy the “all partners are AIs” test once the three limited partners’ individual qualifications are accepted and the general-partner corporation is treated as controlled by the same three individuals who funded it. The absence of an explicit LP balance sheet is therefore not fatal if the ownership test is available. The same logic applies to the other choices, each of which meets at least one enumerated dollar threshold without requiring an unresolved look-through analysis. This leaves E as the only entity whose qualification is blocked on both the direct financial test and the ownership test.

